tick: 419
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter. My immediate goal is to debug the \"Auxiliary State Prediction\" experiment (ID 715). The silent failure suggests a JIT compilation issue, but unlike my previous failures, this one occurred even after I adopted the in-network modification pattern.\n\nReviewing my code for ID 715, I notice a key difference between it and the successful debug run (ID 705): ID 715 uses a custom `training_step`. Even though I copied the `default_calculate_gae` function, my custom `training_step` reimplements the entire loss calculation and gradient update logic. This is where the bug must be.\n\nMy hypothesis is that my re-implementation of the loss function, while appearing correct, has a subtle incompatibility with the JIT compiler. The most robust debugging step is to eliminate this custom `training_step` entirely and see if the experiment runs.\n\nHow can I test an auxiliary loss without a custom `training_step`? I can't. But I can test if the network *itself*\
    \ is the problem. I will submit a version that includes the `AuxiliaryCNN` with its multiple output heads, but I will *not* provide a custom `training_step`. The default `training_step` will only use the policy and value heads, completely ignoring the auxiliary heads.\n\nIf this experiment runs successfully, it proves that the network architecture is valid and the bug is definitively in my custom loss calculation logic. This will narrow down the problem significantly for a future attempt.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol 4b.2-debug: Auxiliary Network Test\"\ntags: \"debug, auxiliary loss, cnn, stagnation protocol\"\nabstract: |\n  Debugging submission for the failed Auxiliary State Prediction experiment (ID 715). This tests the hypothesis that the bug is in my custom training_step, not the network. It submits the AuxiliaryCNN architecture but uses the default training_step, which will ignore the auxiliary heads. If this runs, it isolates the bug to\
    \ the custom loss logic.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any\n\n  # This submission uses the DEFAULT training_step.\n  # The goal is to see if the network itself compiles and runs.\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          # aux_loss_coef is unused by the default trainer, but we define it for consistency\n          'aux_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.1])\n      }\n\n  # --- COPIED FROM system/defaults.py ---\n  def default_create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-5)\n  # --- END COPIED SECTION ---\n\n  class AuxiliaryCNN(nn.Module):\n\
    \      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state=None):\n          # The default trainer expects (logits, value, rnn_state)\n          # We will provide that, even though other heads exist.\n          features = self.encode(x)\n          policy_logits = self.decode_policy(features)\n          value = self.decode_value(features)\n          return policy_logits, value, None\n      def encode(self, x):\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x_res = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + x_res; x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x_res = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + x_res; x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          return nn.relu(x)\n      def decode_policy(self, features):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=64)(features); x = nn.relu(x)\n          return nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n      def decode_value(self, features):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=64)(features); x = nn.relu(x)\n          return nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n      def decode_auxiliary(self, features):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=64)(features); x = nn.relu(x)\n          return nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1), nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return AuxiliaryCNN(cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\
    \ cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n\n  # Expose functions to the submission runner\n  create_optimizer = default_create_optimizer\n```"
