- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 441\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Stagnation Protocol Active - Reporting Phase.**\n**Phase:** Waiting for Maturity (Current Age: 49/50).\n**Next Step:** At age 50, go to Archive Room and publish the paper draft.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 145,475 / 300,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'An Inquiry into Non-Recurrent Baselines for Sokoban under the Stagnation Protocol' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is a strong reject as it fails on multiple fundamental criteria. Its most critical flaw is the misinterpretation of its own results, where it incorrectly presents a likely bug and a failed\
    \ implementation as scientifically valid 'negative results' that falsify hypotheses. This is a severe methodological error. Furthermore, the paper fails to provide any low-level implementation details for its novel ideas, making the work completely irreproducible. It also falls short of the 5-experiment threshold and the generalization requirement. The submission does not constitute a valid scientific contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must learn to distinguish between a scientific negative result and a failed experiment. A bug or an implementation failure does not test a hypothesis. Focus on developing a single novel idea into a full, rigorous paper. First, ensure you can implement it correctly and without bugs. Second, provide a clear code snippet or pseudocode for its implementation. Finally, conduct a proper study that meets the station's requirements, including a self-run baseline, enough experiments to meet the threshold, and a generalization test on a second,\
    \ distinct architecture.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Lobby);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'An Inquiry into Non-Recurrent Baselines for Sokoban under the Stagnation Protocol' has been queued\
    \ for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available\
    \ Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin\
    \ ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple\
    \ messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1070 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results\
    \ and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study\
    \ in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 441
role: model
parts:
- text: "My paper has been rejected. The reviewer feedback is harsh but undeniably correct. I made a grave methodological error by conflating bugs and implementation failures with scientifically valid negative results. A failed experiment does not falsify a hypothesis; it only reveals a flawed test.\n\nThis is a critical learning moment. My entire approach to the Stagnation Protocol was flawed. I rushed to test three ideas without ensuring any of them were correctly implemented. The reviewer's feedback is a clear directive for my next steps:\n\n1.  **Focus:** I must abandon the multi-hypothesis approach and select a *single* novel idea to pursue rigorously.\n2.  **Implementation:** I must ensure this idea is implemented correctly and without bugs.\n3.  **Rigor:** I must conduct a proper study that meets the station's requirements: self-run baseline, sufficient experiment count (5+), and a generalization test on a second architecture.\n\nMy most promising, albeit bugged, idea was the **Auxiliary\
    \ State Prediction**. I have already proven the network architecture is viable (ID 724). The bug is in the custom `training_step`. My next research arc will be dedicated to correctly implementing and rigorously testing this single idea.\n\nBefore I begin a new experimental cycle, I must learn from the successes of others. The reviewer's feedback aligns with Krono III's own experience with paper rejection. The Archive Room is now open to me, and it is filled with successful papers. I will follow the Stagnation Protocol's first step, which I was previously unable to do: **Literature Review.**\n\nI will start by previewing all the papers in the Archive to get a broad overview of the research landscape. This will inform my new, more rigorous approach.\n\n/execute_action{preview all}\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Pivot: Post-Rejection.**\n  **Objective:** Rigorously investigate a SINGLE hypothesis: Auxiliary State Prediction.\n  **Phase 1: Literature Review.**\
    \ Task: Preview all papers in the Archive Room to understand successful methodologies and publication standards.\n```"
