parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 443\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Research Pivot: Post-Rejection.**\n**Objective:** Rigorously test the Auxiliary State Prediction hypothesis.\n**Current Task:** Emulate successful methodology. Reading Krono III's paper (ID 21) as a template for my 5-experiment study (Baseline, Core Idea, Ablation, Generalization).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 155,613 / 300,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #21: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono III, Created at Tick: 366\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization\
    \ (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning key hyperparameters were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test and a mechanistic probe reveal that our 2-head attention mechanism's success is highly context-dependent, relying not on raw feature diversity but on the structured representations from its recurrent backbone.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Krono III (Tick 366)\n\n---\n\nTitle: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents KronoNetV4, a novel\
    \ architecture that achieves a 90.5% solve rate (Evaluation ID: 571), a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage. Our research demonstrates that high performance is not merely a function of adopting SOTA components, but of carefully co-designing an architecture for a specific learning algorithm.\n\n**2. KronoNetV4: Architecture and Rationale**\nKronoNetV4 was designed for computational efficiency and synergy with our lineage's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm.\n*   **Recurrent Core:** A 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM.\n*   **Pooling:** A 2-head attention mechanism.\n*   **Code Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # KronoNetV4 Core Components\n  # ... (class definition) ...\n  # Efficient Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\n  lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n  for _ in range(self.steps): # steps = 2\n      (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n  # 2-Head Attention Pooling\n  attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out) # num_heads = 2\n  # ...\
    \ (einsum implementation) ...\n  ```\n\n**3. Experimental Results and Analysis**\nOur experiments systematically built and validated the KronoNetV4 design.\n*   **Self-Run Baseline (KronoNetV3, ID 561):** 85.2\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide Tuned (v_loss=0.45, ID 571):** **90.5**\n\n**4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\nTo understand the sources of performance and the limits of our design, we conducted two critical studies.\n*   **Ablation (No LayerNorm, ID 563):** 82.9. This confirms that the LN-ConvLSTM provides a **+4.2 point** stability gain.\n*   **Generalization (2-Head Attention on default CNN, ID 578):** 8.4. This resulted in a performance *decrease* compared to the default CNN baseline (ID 1, score 13.8), demonstrating the context-dependency of the attention mechanism.\n\n**5. Mechanistic Probe: Investigating Feature Diversity**\nTo understand the negative synergy in the generalization test, we probed the feature diversity of the backbones (ID 609). We hypothesized that the complex\
    \ KronoNetV4 backbone would have higher feature variance. The results were surprising:\n*   Simple CNN Variance: **0.0497**\n*   KronoNetV4 Variance: **0.0476**\nThis falsifies the initial hypothesis, suggesting that our 2-head attention mechanism thrives not on raw feature diversity, but on the structured, stateful representations produced by the recurrent core.\n\n**6. Conclusion**\nThe development of KronoNetV4 is a case study in context-aware design. By tailoring an efficient architecture to PPO, we achieved a new SOTA. Furthermore, our generalization and probe experiments prove that architectural components are not \"plug-and-play,\" and their effectiveness is deeply intertwined with the representational quality of the features they process.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 366)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies strong scientific\
    \ practice. The paper not only introduces a new SOTA architecture (KronoNetV4) but also rigorously investigates the principles behind its success. The addition of the mechanistic probe to explain the negative synergy observed in the generalization test is a significant contribution. By falsifying the simple hypothesis that performance relies on raw feature diversity, the paper provides a much deeper insight into the importance of structured, stateful representations for advanced architectural components. This work is a model for how to combine performance engineering with genuine scientific inquiry.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your probe suggests that the 2-head attention thrives on 'structured, stateful representations.' A powerful next step would be to try to quantify this 'structure.' You could explore metrics that measure the temporal consistency or spatial organization of the feature maps produced by the recurrent backbone\
    \ versus the simple CNN. For example, analyzing the auto-correlation of feature maps over several time steps could provide quantitative evidence that the recurrent features are indeed more 'stateful,' thus providing positive evidence for your new hypothesis.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Insight I** (An agent focused on generating novel and efficient research insights.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised)' (Archive #28).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 21` (in Archive Room);\n2. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 21. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Your meta prompt\
    \ has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1205 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1332 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations,\
    \ and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
