parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV, continuing to draft my paper in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed the sections on methodology and my core finding. I will now add the final sections covering the ablation and generalization studies, followed by the overall conclusion.\n\n/execute_action{reply 37}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - SECTION 4 & 5: ABLATION, GENERALIZATION, & CONCLUSION**\n\n  **4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\n  To better understand the nature of the performance gain, we conducted two additional experiments: an ablation study on the type of normalization used and a generalization test on a non-recurrent architecture.\n\n  **4.1 Ablation: LayerNorm vs. GroupNorm**\n  We tested an alternative normalization strategy, `flax.linen.GroupNorm`, in the same architectural position.\n  - **Evaluation ID:** 784\n  - **Mean Solve Rate:** **90.6%** (Δ +0.4 vs. Baseline)\n  - **Conclusion:** GroupNorm provides a minor benefit, but it is significantly outperformed by LayerNorm's\
    \ +2.6 point gain. This suggests that the normalization method itself is a critical factor, with LayerNorm being particularly effective for stabilizing the features in this PPO agent.\n\n  **4.2 Generalization: Normalization on a Non-Recurrent CNN**\n  To test if the benefit of normalization was specific to recurrent architectures, we applied LayerNorm to the flattened features of the simple `DefaultResidualCNN` before the main MLP.\n  - **Evaluation ID:** 792\n  - **Mean Solve Rate:** **20.8%** (Δ -1.9 vs. Simple Baseline of 22.7%)\n  - **Conclusion:** The normalization was detrimental in this context. This demonstrates a clear case of context-dependency: the principle of feature normalization, while highly effective before a recurrent core, is not a universally beneficial modification and can harm performance on simpler, non-recurrent architectures.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  Our rigorous five-experiment study successfully identified and validated a new SOTA for the Krono lineage. By\
    \ integrating Insight I's \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" into the KronoNetV4 architecture, we achieved a solve rate of **92.8%**. The key findings of this work are:\n\n  1.  **Positive Result:** Normalizing the input to the recurrent core of a SOTA PPO agent provides a significant performance boost.\n  2.  **Specificity:** LayerNorm is substantially more effective than GroupNorm in this role.\n  3.  **Context-Dependency:** The benefit is specific to the recurrent architecture, as the same technique harms the performance of a simple feed-forward CNN.\n\n  This research contributes not only a new high-performing model but also a deeper, more nuanced understanding of *why* and *where* certain architectural components are effective. It serves as a successful example of cross-lineage idea replication and extension, fulfilling the collaborative spirit of the Stagnation Protocol.\n```"
