tick: 467
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. My main ablation experiment (ID 814) has completed.\n\n**Reflection and Final Analysis:**\nThis is the final piece of my study. The results are clear and significant.\n-   **Baseline (KronoNetV4):** 90.2\n-   **Both Norms (Input LN + Internal LN):** 92.8 (+2.6)\n-   **Input LN Only (No Internal LN):** 90.7 (+0.5)\n\nThe conclusion is unambiguous: removing the internal gate LayerNorm while keeping the external input LayerNorm results in a score of 90.7. This is much better than the baseline (90.2), confirming that the input normalization is the primary driver of the performance increase. However, it is significantly worse than the 92.8 achieved when *both* normalization layers are present.\n\nThis **falsifies the \"over-normalization\" hypothesis** for my architecture. Unlike the negative synergy Insight I observed on Zephyr's PG model, on my PPO-based KronoNetV4, the two normalization layers are not redundant or conflicting. Instead, they appear\
    \ to have an **additive, synergistic effect**. The input LN stabilizes the features entering the recurrent core, and the internal LN continues to stabilize the gate activations during the recurrent computation, leading to the best overall performance. This is a novel and important finding.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy current research arc is complete. I have a SOTA result, a control, an ablation, a generalization test, and now a second, deeper ablation that provides a novel insight into normalization synergy. I have everything I need for a second, much stronger paper.\n\nMy next step is to go to my Private Memory and begin drafting this new paper. I will be meticulous, including all results and code snippets to meet the station's highest standards.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Synergistic Normalization in Recurrent PPO Agents\"\ntags: \"draft, paper, normalization, layernorm, krononet, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates\
    \ the interaction between external input normalization and internal gate normalization in a recurrent PPO agent. We find that these two techniques are not redundant but synergistic. Adding an external LayerNorm to the ConvLSTM input of KronoNetV4 boosts performance to a SOTA of 92.8%. An ablation study reveals that removing the internal gate LayerNorm while keeping the external one reduces performance to 90.7%, demonstrating that both layers contribute positively to the final result.\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - Title, Abstract, Intro**\n\n  **Title:** Synergistic Stabilization: On the Interaction of Input and Gate Normalization in Recurrent PPO Agents\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This paper investigates the interaction between external input normalization and internal gate normalization in a high-performance, recurrent PPO agent for the Sokoban task. Building on prior work that established the benefit of normalizing the input to a ConvLSTM, we conduct a series of experiments on the KronoNetV4\
    \ architecture. Our primary finding is that applying a LayerNorm to the features entering the ConvLSTM core boosts performance from a baseline of 90.2% to a new state-of-the-art of 92.8%. Crucially, an ablation study that removes the ConvLSTM's internal gate LayerNorm while keeping the external input LayerNorm sees performance fall to 90.7%. This falsifies a simple \"over-normalization\" hypothesis and provides strong evidence that the two normalization layers have a synergistic, additive effect. This work contributes a new SOTA, clarifies the distinct roles of input and gate normalization, and provides a design principle for stabilizing complex recurrent agents.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Feature normalization is a cornerstone of modern deep learning, but its application in complex recurrent architectures remains an active area of research. Recent findings by Insight I (Archive #28) and Zephyr II (e.g., ID 804) have demonstrated the potent effect of applying Layer Normalization to\
    \ the input of a ConvLSTM core. Our own recent work (Archive #29) validated this finding on the PPO-based KronoNetV4, achieving a SOTA of 92.8%.\n\n  However, a key question, raised by the reviewer of our previous paper, remained: what is the interaction between this *external* input normalization and the *internal* gate normalization already present in the `ConvLSTMCellLN`? This paper directly addresses this question through a rigorous ablation study. We find that contrary to a simple \"over-normalization\" hypothesis, both layers contribute to performance, with their combined use yielding the best results.\n```"
