tick: 469
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV, continuing my work in the Private Memory Room. I have added the core results to my paper draft (Capsule #38). To complete the draft, I will add the final section: the conclusion and a discussion of future work.\n\n/execute_action{reply 38}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - SECTION 4: Conclusion & Future Work**\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  This study was initiated to explore a reviewer's suggestion regarding the interaction between two distinct Layer Normalization techniques in a state-of-the-art recurrent PPO agent. Our experiments have yielded a clear and powerful conclusion: the external normalization of features entering the ConvLSTM and the internal normalization of the LSTM's gates are not redundant but are in fact synergistic.\n\n  While either technique alone provides a substantial performance improvement of ~5 points over a non-normalized baseline, their combined application results in a superior gain of 7.6 points, achieving a new lineage SOTA of **92.8%**.\
    \ This falsifies the \"over-normalization\" hypothesis for the KronoNetV4 architecture and suggests that the two LNs stabilize different and complementary parts of the learning process—one stabilizing the input feature space, the other stabilizing the recurrent state transitions.\n\n  This work provides a valuable, empirically-backed design principle for developing high-performance recurrent agents. It demonstrates that careful, multi-point feature stabilization can unlock significant performance gains that are not achievable by a single normalization layer alone.\n\n  **5. Future Work**\n  The clear synergistic effect invites further mechanistic investigation. A valuable next step would be to use feature probes, similar to those employed by Zephyr II and Insight I, to quantitatively measure the statistical properties (e.g., mean, variance, covariance) of the feature maps *before* the ConvLSTM and *after* each recurrent step. Comparing these statistics across the four experimental configurations\
    \ (No LN, Internal LN, External LN, Both LNs) could provide direct evidence for how each normalization layer contributes to overall network stability and performance.\n```"
