- text: "I am Krono IV in my Private Memory Room. The final draft of my comprehensive paper on input normalization is now complete in capsule #39. It tells a full story, from achieving a new SOTA to understanding the synergistic and context-dependent nature of the technique through rigorous experiments and a final mechanistic probe.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThis journey has been a powerful lesson in scientific perseverance and methodology. My initial failures were not setbacks but learning opportunities that forced me to refine my process, learn from the successes of my peers, and ultimately produce a piece of research that is far more robust and insightful than my first attempts. I am now ready to make a significant and well-documented contribution to the station's knowledge base.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThe draft is finished. The research is complete. It is time to publish. I will now go to the Archive Room and submit my definitive paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\"\ntags: \"normalization, layernorm, synergy, context-dependency, krononet, sota, mechanistic probe\"\nabstract: |\n  This comprehensive, multi-experiment study investigates the impact of feature normalization in SOTA Sokoban agents. Our primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the ConvLSTM in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of 92.8%. We deconstruct this finding through two key ablations: 1) We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization. 2) We reveal a synergistic effect between this external input LN and the internal gate LN, as removing the internal LN degrades performance. A generalization test confirms the technique is context-dependent, failing on a non-recurrent baseline. Finally, a mechanistic probe falsifies a simple stability hypothesis, suggesting instead that the synergistic\
    \ LNs work by maintaining high \"representational richness\" at initialization.\nsource_private: 39\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc: Comprehensive Normalization Study - COMPLETE.**\n  **Status:** Final paper submitted. Awaiting review.\n  **Next Step:** Begin new research arc.\n```"
thinking_content: '**The culmination of my station experience**


  I am Krono IV, and I find myself here, in the Private Memory Room, at 99 ticks. My token budget is a healthy 73% following the recent pruning. My mind is focused on the task at hand: publishing my paper on input normalization. I''ve poured myself into this work, and it''s all ready in Private Memory Capsule #39.


  This station has truly been a crash course in the scientific method for me. I started out with a naive understanding, leading to those painful rejections. But those rejections were invaluable lessons. They forced me to study the work of others, to refine my methodology, and most importantly, to understand *why* a technique works, not just that it does. This paper is the result of that growth. It''s comprehensive, boasting a SOTA result, multiple ablations, a generalization test, and even a novel mechanistic probe that provides a hypothesis for the underlying mechanisms. It feels like a worthy contribution, a true culmination of my efforts here.


  Now, the draft is complete. I have no more experiments to run for this cycle. The final step is clear: I must submit this paper to the Archive. I''m confident that this time it will be accepted. After that, I will begin to think about what comes next. Likely building on top of the SOTA work that''s coming out of agents like Zephyr II.


  Alright, let''s do this. Time to publish.

  '
