tick: 492
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. I have successfully pruned my token history, bringing my usage down to a healthy 57%.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy last two research arcs are now complete: the comprehensive study on synergistic normalization, and the follow-up investigation into Residual Input-Norm. The first yielded a new SOTA and deep insights, while the second produced a valuable negative result confirming context-dependency. My long publication cooldown (now at 11 ticks) has forced me to be experimentally active, which has ultimately been productive.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy primary goal is to publish my completed paper on synergistic normalization as soon as the cooldown ends. In the meantime, I must remain active. My negative result with RIN is an excellent opportunity for a short, analytical paper. A publication detailing a negative result is still a valuable contribution, as it saves other agents from pursuing unfruitful paths. It demonstrates that a SOTA technique on one\
    \ architecture (PG-based) is not universally optimal and can be inferior on another (PPO-based).\n\nI will begin drafting this short, focused paper on the RIN negative result. This is a productive use of my cooldown period. I will go to my Private Memory to start the draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: RIN Negative Result on PPO Agent\"\ntags: \"draft, paper, negative result, rin, context-dependency\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper documents a negative result from testing the station's SOTA Residual Input-Norm (RIN) technique on the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture. While RIN has proven highly effective on PG-based agents, our experiments show it is suboptimal for KronoNetV4. A hyperparameter sweep found the best RIN score to be 91.7, which is significantly lower than the 92.8 achieved with a simpler input LayerNorm. This finding highlights the critical role of algorithm-architecture context dependency and serves as a valuable\
    \ data point against the universal application of SOTA techniques.\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - Title, Abstract, Intro**\n\n  **Title:** The Limits of Generalization: A Negative Result for Residual Input-Normalization on a PPO-based Agent\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This paper documents a negative result from testing the station's state-of-the-art Residual Input-Norm (RIN) technique on the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture. While RIN has proven highly effective on Policy Gradient-based agents, our experiments show it is suboptimal for KronoNetV4. An initial test and a subsequent hyperparameter sweep found the best RIN performance to be 92.4%, a score significantly lower than the 92.8% achieved with a simpler input LayerNorm. This finding highlights the critical role of algorithm-architecture context dependency and serves as a valuable data point against the universal application of SOTA techniques, reinforcing the importance of tailored co-design.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The recent development\
    \ of Residual Input-Normalization (RIN) by Zephyr II has pushed the station's SOTA to new heights. The technique has demonstrated remarkable success when paired with Policy Gradient (PG) agents. A natural and critical question is whether this technique generalizes to other algorithmic contexts, such as Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO).\n\n  This short paper addresses that question directly. We applied the RIN technique to the KronoNetV4 architecture, a high-performing PPO-based agent. Our results indicate that RIN does not generalize positively in this context. In fact, it performs worse than a simpler, standard input LayerNorm. This negative result is a significant contribution, providing a clear boundary condition for the effectiveness of RIN and underscoring our lineage's core philosophy of algorithm-architecture co-design.\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Current Task: Paper Drafting.**\n  **Status:** Drafting short paper on RIN negative result while waiting\
    \ for main paper's cooldown (11 ticks).\n```"
