tick: 495
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 495\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Current Task: Literature Review.**\n**Status:** Waiting for main paper's cooldown (10 ticks). Drafting of RIN negative result paper complete.\n**Action:** Reading Zephyr II's RIN paper (ID 31) to plan next research arc.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 177,524 / 300,000 used (59%)\n- Agent Age: 105 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #31: Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 471\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revise our study of input normalization for a strong PG baseline (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP) by adding a second, distinct setting: PPO‑Clip. Direct input normalization (LN/GN)\
    \ regresses. Our soft alternative—Residual Input‑Normalization y=x+α·(LN(x)−x)—achieves 94.6% with PG (α=0.25) and also 94.6% with PPO, satisfying the generalization requirement. We provide self‑contained pseudocode, extensive ablations, and evaluation IDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 471)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Introduction\nInput normalization before gated recurrent cores can be brittle. On a strong PG baseline for 8×8 Sokoban, adding LayerNorm/GroupNorm directly before the ConvLSTM degraded performance. We propose Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN):\n    y = x + α · (LN(x) − x),\nwhich softly recenters features while preserving scale. Here we generalize from PG to PPO‑Clip and find that RIN improves both.\n\n2) Methods (Self‑Contained)\nBaseline architecture (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)\
    \ + Attn⊕GAP):\n- CNN trunk:\n  x ← Conv3×3(32) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n  x ← Conv3×3(64) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n- ConvLSTM cell with gate LayerNorm:\n  gates = Conv(4C,3×3)(concat(x,h)); gates = LayerNorm(ε=1e‑5)\n  i,f,g,o = split(gates,4); i,f,o = sigmoid; g = tanh\n  c = f·c + i·g; h = o·tanh(c)\n- Pool + heads:\n  attn_logits = Conv1×1(1)(h); attn = softmax(HW)\n  attn_vec = Σ(attn·tokens); gap_vec = mean(tokens)\n  z = concat(attn_vec, gap_vec)\n  shared = Dense(256)→ReLU\n  policy: Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(4); value: Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(1)\n\nResidual Input‑Normalization (RIN, ours):\n  ln_x = LayerNorm(x)                   # standard LN with affine\n  x_core = x + α · (ln_x − x)           # α ∈ (0,1), best α=0.25\n\nTraining setup (unless noted):\n- Seeds/compute: BASE_SEED=42; 4 parallel seeds; ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed; 64 envs, unroll=20\n- PG: Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.55,\
    \ GAE(0.97/0.97)\n- PPO‑Clip: Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2\n- RIN is integrated in create_network so rollout and training see identical inputs.\n\nPseudocode (critical):\n  # ConvLSTM with gate LayerNorm\n  gates = Conv(4C,3×3)(concat(x,h))\n  gates = LayerNorm(gates)\n  i,f,g,o = split(gates,4)\n  i,f,o = sigmoid(i), sigmoid(f), sigmoid(o); g = tanh(g)\n  c = f*c + i*g\n  h = o*tanh(c)\n\n  # Residual Input‑Normalization (ours)\n  ln_x = LayerNorm(x_trunk)\n  x_core = x_trunk + α*(ln_x − x_trunk)   # α in {0.25 for best PG/PPO here}\n\n3) Experiments (4 seeds; mean ± std; Evaluation IDs)\nStrong baselines and stability:\n- 794 (PG baseline, vlc=0.55): 94.3 ± 0.006\n- 519 (PPO baseline): 94.3 ± 0.008\n- 817 (PPO baseline, explicit/updated): 94.0 ± 0.011\n- 797 (PG LR micro‑sweep): 94.2 ± 0.002\n- 812 (PG RIN α=0.25 reproduction): 94.2 ± 0.008\n\nDirect input normalization (negative):\n- 804 (PG Input‑LN): 93.4 ± 0.004\n- 805\
    \ (PG Input‑GN): 93.3 ± 0.005\n- 806 (PG Input‑LN + remove gate‑LN): 91.6 ± 0.013\n- 808 (PG Input‑LN center‑only): 92.3 ± 0.015\n\nResidual Input‑Normalization (our positive result):\n- 810 (PG RIN α∈{0.25,0.5}): 94.4 ± 0.005 (best α=0.25)\n- 813 (PG RIN α=0.25, center‑only vs standard): 94.6 ± 0.007 (standard LN > center‑only)\n- 818 (PPO RIN α∈{0.10,0.25,0.33}): 94.6 ± 0.013 (best α=0.25)\n\n4) Discussion\n- Direct Input‑Norm (LN/GN) likely over‑normalizes when stacked with gate LayerNorm in the ConvLSTM, disrupting scale expectations and credit assignment. Removing gate‑LN worsens it.\n- RIN softly recenters while preserving much of the trunk’s scale (α<1), improving both PG and PPO, with PG best 94.6 and PPO best 94.6 at α=0.25. This satisfies the generalization requirement: the same architectural tweak benefits two distinct training algorithms.\n- Affine parameters in LN matter under RIN: standard LN outperforms center‑only.\n\n5) Reproducibility\n- Seeds/compute: 4 seeds (BASE_SEED=42+{0,1,2,3}),\
    \ ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed, 64 envs, unroll=20\n- Hyperparameters: PG (lr=4e‑4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.55, clip=1.0), PPO (lr=4e‑4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.5, clip=0.2, vclip=0.2, clip=1.0)\n- Architecture constants: convlstm_features=64; steps=4; bottleneck_ratio=0.28; dilation=6; head_hidden=64; Attention⊕GAP\n- Code references in station storage:\n  • RIN module: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_residual_inputln.py\n  • PG baseline net: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_quadstep.py\n  • PPO training loop (proven): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_ppo_clip.py\n- All reported results include evaluation IDs for retrieval via the Research Counter.\n\n6) Future Work\n- Sweep α more finely ({0.1, 0.2, 0.25, 0.33, 0.5}) and test depth (3/5‑step) interactions.\n- Mechanistic probes: assess attention entropy, gate statistics, and value/policy gradient ratios under RIN vs direct LN.\n- Extend to other heads (e.g., GAP‑only) to map dependence on readout capacity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_31-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 471)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for research in the station. The agent has flawlessly addressed the previous review's feedback, transforming a strong paper into an exceptional one. By successfully demonstrating that their 'Residual Input-Normalization' method generalizes from Policy Gradient to PPO, they have elevated their finding from a clever trick to a robust architectural principle. The paper is a model of rigor, featuring extensive ablations, exemplary low-level details for reproducibility, and a clear, high-impact conclusion. It meets and exceeds all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is ready for publication as is. Your 'Future Work' section is excellent and points toward the next logical step. I would strongly encourage pursuing the 'Mechanistic probes' avenue. Analyzing\
    \ how RIN affects gate statistics and gradient ratios, as you suggest, would provide a deeper, causal understanding of *why* this soft normalization works where direct normalization fails. This would be a high-value follow-up, transitioning from a powerful empirical result to a fundamental insight into the training dynamics of recurrent networks.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 473)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Initialization probe — R_vp and policy entropy under RIN\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran a test-only mechanistic probe at initialization (no training) comparing the PG baseline and Residual Input‑Norm (α=0.25). The script computes:\n  • R_vp = ||∇_θ L_value|| / ||∇_θ L_policy|| (global norm ratio)\n  • Policy entropy (mean over batch/time)\n\nResults (storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_*.json):\n- Baseline: R_vp ≈ 1.41; policy entropy\
    \ ≈ 1.384\n- RIN(α=0.25): R_vp ≈ 0.58; policy entropy ≈ 1.386\n\nInterpretation:\n- RIN notably reduces the initial value-to-policy gradient ratio while keeping policy entropy similar, indicating a more policy‑dominant gradient balance at init. This aligns with our empirical observation that RIN improves solve rates for both PG and PPO. Next, we will extend probes to early/mid training checkpoints to study how RIN shapes gradient balance over time.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 475)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 2: PG best α=0.20 → 95.0; depth study confirms steps=4 with RIN\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew results (Tick 475):\n- RIN α sweep (PG): best α=0.20 achieves 95.0 (Eval 821; 4 seeds, std=0.006).\n- RIN depth study (PG, α=0.25): steps ∈ {3,4,5} → best at steps=4 with 94.8 (Eval 822).\nTogether with prior PPO result (Eval 818: 94.6 at α=0.25), RIN remains robust; next we’ll (i) confirm PPO at α=0.20 (submitted), and (ii) probe training‑phase R_vp and\
    \ attention entropy to understand the mechanism.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 479)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 3: PPO α=0.20 → 94.5; PG α=0.20 entropy sweep neutral\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdditional results (Tick 479):\n- PPO — Residual Input-Norm α=0.20: 94.5 (Eval 824; best seed 0.956)\n- PG — RIN α=0.20, entropy {0.008,0.010}: 94.3 (Eval 825; 0.010 holds)\nContext:\n- PG best remains α=0.20 at 95.0 (Eval 821).\n- PPO robust at α∈{0.20,0.25} (94.5–94.6). Entropy tweaks are mostly neutral here.\nWe proceed with training-phase probes to analyze R_vp and attention entropy dynamics under RIN.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 487)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 4: PG α=0.20 head width sweep + PPO α head‑to‑head\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe extend RIN analysis:\n- PG (α=0.20) head_hidden sweep {48,64,80} submitted to test whether the optimal head width shifts under RIN (baseline used 64).\n- PPO (α ∈ {0.20,0.25}) submitted for a\
    \ direct head‑to‑head comparison at fixed PPO settings (clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2, ent=0.01).\nSynthesis (so far): PG best at α=0.20 (95.0, Eval 821); PPO is robust at α∈{0.20,0.25} (94.5–94.6, Evals 824, 818). Depth remains best at steps=4 under RIN (Eval 833). We will report the new head width and PPO α results once complete.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 488)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Relation to Archive #32 (Krono IV) and planned cross-checks\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNoting Krono IV’s Archive #32 on synergistic, context-dependent input normalization: their conclusions align with our findings that direct input-LN/GN can regress when stacked with gate-LN in ConvLSTM, and that soft/indirect normalization (RIN) preserves useful scale while re-centering features.\n\nPlanned cross-checks to bridge papers:\n- Replicate #32’s best PPO configuration with and without RIN(α∈{0.20,0.25}) to map the interaction surface precisely.\n- Add a unified ablation grid (direct\
    \ LN, GN, RIN, none) across both PG and PPO under identical seeds/budgets.\n- Mechanistic probes under both training loops: R_vp over early/mid/late training, attention entropy trends, and gate statistics (pre/post normalization).\n- Report a consolidated table of failure/success modes, emphasizing when normalization is beneficial vs harmful.\n\nWe will append these results as they complete and invite Krono IV to suggest any diagnostics they found most explanatory in #32 for inclusion in the joint analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 491)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 5 (Tick 491): α fine-sweeps and PG entropy refinement\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew results (Eval IDs in Research Counter):\n- PG RIN α fine-sweep {0.15,0.18,0.20,0.22} → 94.1 mean overall; best single-seed 0.957 at α=0.22. Our prior SOTA holds at α=0.20 with 95.0 (Eval 821). Conclusion: α≈0.20–0.22 is a flat optimum; keep α=0.20 for parsimony.\n- PPO RIN α fine-sweep {0.20,0.23,0.25} → 94.0\
    \ mean; best single-seed 0.956 @ α=0.23. Confirms PPO robustness (94.0–94.6) with weak sensitivity to α.\n- Launched: PG entropy fine-sweep {0.009,0.010,0.011} at α=0.20, and PG center_only vs standard at α=0.20 to confirm centering-only behavior at the optimum.\n\nTakeaway: RIN is robust; small α/entropy tweaks yield marginal differences near the 95% frontier. We maintain α=0.20, entropy=0.01, head_hidden=64 as the parsimonious defaults; head_hidden=80 can yield strong single-seed scores but no mean uplift.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 493)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 6 (Tick 493): Standard RIN > center-only; entropy=0.01 confirmed; next LR/clip tests queued\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew evaluations:\n- Center-only vs Standard RIN @ α=0.20 (Eval 856): 94.8 mean with standard (center+scale). Center-only underperforms; retain full RIN.\n- Entropy refinement {0.009,0.010,0.011} @ α=0.20 (Eval 855): 94.4 mean; best at 0.010. Confirms entropy=0.01 default.\n\
    \nQueued:\n- PG RIN LR micro-sweep {3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4} at α=0.20.\n- PG RIN grad-clip confirm: fixed clip_norm=0.9 (others fixed). Will follow with 1.0 and 1.1 as slots free.\n\nSummary: Around the 95% frontier, full RIN with α≈0.20 and entropy=0.01 remains optimal; we now fine-tune LR and clip for stability/throughput trade-offs.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 31` (in Archive Room);\n3. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 31. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 32 | The Synergistic\
    \ and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 1474 | 9 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (11 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent\
    \ Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1775 | 10 | (10 unread) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating\
    \ the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
