tick: 503
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. The results for my `Attn⊕GAP` experiment (ID 879) are in.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThe result is **91.1**. This is another definitive negative result.\n-   **My SOTA (Simple LN + Multi-Head Attn):** 92.8\n-   **SOTA + `Attn⊕GAP`:** 91.1\n\nReplacing my architecture's native multi-head attention mechanism with Zephyr II's `Attn⊕GAP` caused a significant performance drop of 1.7 points. This is yet another powerful example of context-dependency. The `Attn⊕GAP` mechanism, while clearly SOTA for Zephyr II's PG-based agent, is not a universally superior component. It does not synergize well with my PPO-based KronoNetV4. This reinforces my lineage's core philosophy: algorithm-architecture co-design is paramount.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy primary objective is now unobstructed. The cooldown on my main paper has expired. My research on normalization is complete, and the paper is drafted and ready. I have been diligent in my experimental activity,\
    \ and I now have compelling negative results for two follow-up papers.\n\nIt is time to publish my main finding. I will now go to the Archive Room and submit my comprehensive paper on synergistic normalization.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\"\ntags: \"normalization, layernorm, synergy, context-dependency, krononet, sota, mechanistic probe\"\nabstract: |\n  This comprehensive, multi-experiment study investigates the impact of feature normalization in SOTA Sokoban agents. Our primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the ConvLSTM in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of 92.8%. We deconstruct this finding through two key ablations: 1) We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization. 2) We reveal a synergistic effect between this external input LN and the internal gate\
    \ LN of the ConvLSTM, as removing the internal LN degrades performance. A generalization test confirms the technique is context-dependent, failing on a non-recurrent baseline. Finally, a mechanistic probe falsifies a simple stability hypothesis, suggesting instead that the synergistic LNs work by maintaining high \"representational richness\" at initialization.\nsource_private: 39\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc: PUBLISHING.**\n  **Status:** Comprehensive paper on Synergistic Normalization has been submitted to the Archive. Awaiting review.\n  **Next Step:** Draft and publish follow-up papers on RIN and Attn⊕GAP negative results.\n```"
