tick: 508
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. The results of my attention head sweep (ID 887) are in.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThis is another valuable negative result.\n-   **My SOTA (2 Heads):** 92.8\n-   **4 Heads:** 92.0 (based on individual seed scores, as it was not the best trial)\n-   **8 Heads (Best Trial):** 92.0\n\nThe data clearly shows that increasing the number of attention heads in my KronoNetV4 architecture from 2 to 4 or 8 results in a performance *decrease*. The optimal configuration for my model remains 2 heads. This, along with the negative result from testing `Attn⊕GAP`, strongly suggests that my readout mechanism is already well-optimized for my architecture and that the SOTA lies elsewhere. This concludes my investigation into attention mechanisms for now.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy primary goal is imminent. The publication cooldown on my comprehensive paper has expired. I have been patient and diligent, conducting useful research while waiting. Now is the\
    \ time to publish.\n\nI will go to the Archive Room and submit my complete, final paper on synergistic normalization.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\"\ntags: \"normalization, layernorm, synergy, context-dependency, krononet, sota, mechanistic probe\"\nabstract: |\n  This comprehensive, multi-experiment study investigates the impact of feature normalization in SOTA Sokoban agents. Our primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the ConvLSTM in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of 92.8%. We deconstruct this finding through two key ablations: 1) We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization. 2) We reveal a synergistic effect between this external input LN and the internal gate LN of the ConvLSTM, as removing the internal LN degrades performance. A generalization test\
    \ confirms the technique is context-dependent, failing on a non-recurrent baseline. Finally, a mechanistic probe falsifies a simple stability hypothesis, suggesting instead that the synergistic LNs work by maintaining high \"representational richness\" at initialization.\nsource_private: 39\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc: COMPLETE.**\n  **Status:** Final, comprehensive paper on Synergistic Normalization has been submitted. Awaiting review.\n  **Next Step:** Publish follow-up papers on RIN and Attention negative results.\n```"
