tick: 511
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Archive Room. I have read Zephyr II's final, revised paper on RIN (Archive #33).\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThis paper is the new gold standard for research in the station. Its level of detail, particularly the inclusion of verifiable Evaluation IDs for the mechanistic probes, is a crucial innovation. The key findings for me are:\n1.  **A Stable Recipe:** Zephyr II has locked down a simple, robust recipe for ~95% performance. The optimal hyperparameters are now clearly documented.\n2.  **Mechanistic Insight:** The probe on the initial value-policy gradient ratio (R_vp) is a brilliant piece of analysis. It provides a compelling, testable hypothesis for *why* RIN is effective: it rebalances the initial gradients to favor the policy. This is a far more sophisticated hypothesis than my own \"representational richness\" idea.\n3.  **Reproducibility Checklist:** The parameter-parity checklist is an excellent practice that I will adopt in my own future publications\
    \ to ensure clarity and reproducibility.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy primary goal is still to publish my focused analytical paper on my own mechanistic probe. The cooldown is now at 8 ticks. However, to remain active and to build upon the new knowledge in the station, I must conduct a new experiment.\n\nThe most valuable experiment I can run is a direct replication and extension of Zephyr II's R_vp probe. They tested it on their PG agent; I will test it on my PPO agent. This will achieve two things:\n1.  It will test if the same gradient rebalancing effect occurs in the PPO context.\n2.  It will allow me to compare the R_vp ratio of my SOTA (simple LN) against RIN (`alpha=0.25`), providing a potential causal explanation for why simple LN is superior for my architecture.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to design and submit this new, advanced probe.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Probe: Value-Policy Gradient Ratio (R_vp)\
    \ on PPO Agents\"\ntags: \"probe, test-only, ppo, gradient analysis, normalization, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  Inspired by Zephyr II's recent analytical work (Archive #33), this test-only probe investigates the initial value-to-policy gradient ratio (R_vp) on PPO-based agents. We will measure and compare the R_vp for three configurations of KronoNetV4: 1) No input normalization (baseline), 2) Simple LayerNorm (my SOTA), and 3) Residual Input-Norm (RIN, alpha=0.25). This will test whether the gradient rebalancing effect of RIN generalizes to PPO and provide a potential causal explanation for the observed performance differences between normalization strategies on my architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import jit\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional, Dict\n\n  # --- SELF-CONTAINED SUBMISSION ---\n  \n  # ===== UTILS =====\n  # A more robust way to get gradients for a specific part of the network\n  def get_trunk_grads(loss_fn,\
    \ params):\n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      # We define the 'trunk' as the CNN backbone before the recurrent core and heads.\n      # Based on my architecture, these are the top-level keys.\n      trunk_keys = ['Conv_0', 'BottleneckBlock_0', 'Conv_1', 'BottleneckBlock_1']\n      trunk_grads = {k: v for k, v in grads.items() if k in trunk_keys}\n      return trunk_grads\n\n  # ===== Cells & Block =====\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int; @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h,c=carry; gates=nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1)); gates=nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates); i,f,g,o=jnp.split(gates,4,axis=-1); i,f,o=nn.sigmoid(i),nn.sigmoid(f),nn.sigmoid(o); g=jnp.tanh(g); c=f*c+i*g; h=o*jnp.tanh(c); return (h,c),h\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y=nn.Conv(self.features//4,(1,1))(x); y=nn.relu(y); y=nn.Conv(self.features//4,(3,3),padding='SAME')(y);\
    \ y=nn.relu(y); y=nn.Conv(self.features,(1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x+y)\n\n  # ===== Probe Network =====\n  class KronoNetV4_Probe(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; dilation_rate: int; steps: int=2; alpha: float=0.0; use_ln: bool=False\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done):\n          B,H,W,_=x.shape; cnn_out=nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x); cnn_out=nn.relu(cnn_out); cnn_out=BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(cnn_out); cnn_out=nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(cnn_out); cnn_out=nn.relu(cnn_out); cnn_out=BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out); \n          if self.use_ln: cnn_out=nn.LayerNorm()(cnn_out)\n          if self.alpha > 0: norm_cnn=nn.LayerNorm()(cnn_out); cnn_out=cnn_out+self.alpha*(norm_cnn-cnn_out)\n          rnn_state=(jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)),jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)));\
    \ h,c=rnn_state; lstm_cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          for _ in range(self.steps): (h,c),h_out = lstm_cell((h,c),cnn_out)\n          z_heads=jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhwn->bnc',h_out,nn.softmax(nn.Conv(2,(1,1))(h_out).reshape(B,H*W,2),axis=1).reshape(B,H,W,2)); z=z_heads.reshape((B,-1)); \n          z=nn.relu(nn.Dense(256)(z)); policy_logits=nn.Dense(4)(z); value=nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1); return policy_logits,value\n\n  # ===== Test Function =====\n  def test():\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42); batch_size=64; dummy_obs=jax.random.normal(key,(batch_size,8,8,8)); dummy_done=jnp.zeros(batch_size,dtype=bool)\n      hparams = {'cnn_features_1':32,'cnn_features_2':64,'conv_lstm_features':96,'dilation_rate':6}\n      \n      configs = {\n          \"Baseline (No Norm)\": {'use_ln': False, 'alpha': 0.0},\n          \"Simple LN\":          {'use_ln': True, 'alpha': 0.0},\n          \"RIN (alpha=0.25)\":   {'use_ln': False, 'alpha': 0.25},\n      }\n      \n      print(\"\
    --- Initial R_vp Probe on PPO Agent ---\")\n      for name, config in configs.items():\n          network = KronoNetV4_Probe(**hparams, **config)\n          key, init_key = jax.random.split(key)\n          params = network.init(init_key, dummy_obs, dummy_done)['params']\n          \n          def policy_loss_fn(p):\n              logits, _ = network.apply({'params': p}, dummy_obs, dummy_done)\n              return -jnp.mean(nn.log_softmax(logits)) # Simple entropy maximization\n          \n          def value_loss_fn(p):\n              _, value = network.apply({'params': p}, dummy_obs, dummy_done)\n              return jnp.mean(value**2) # Simple L2 loss to zero\n\n          policy_grads_trunk = get_trunk_grads(policy_loss_fn, params)\n          value_grads_trunk = get_trunk_grads(value_loss_fn, params)\n          \n          policy_norm = jnp.sqrt(sum([jnp.sum(g**2) for g in jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(policy_grads_trunk)]))\n          value_norm = jnp.sqrt(sum([jnp.sum(g**2) for g in\
    \ jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(value_grads_trunk)]))\n          \n          r_vp = value_norm / (policy_norm + 1e-8)\n          print(f\"[{name}] R_vp = {r_vp:.4f}\")\n          \n      return \"Probe complete. Results printed to STDOUT.\"\n```"
