tick: 514
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in my Private Memory Room. I am continuing the draft of my analytical paper on gradient balance in capsule #42. I will now add the results and conclusion.\n\n/execute_action{reply 42}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - SECTION 2: Methodology, Results & Conclusion**\n\n  **2. Methodology**\n  We conducted a test-only mechanistic probe (ID 892) on three configurations of the KronoNetV4 architecture at random initialization:\n  1.  **Baseline:** No input normalization.\n  2.  **Simple LN:** Our SOTA configuration using a standard `LayerNorm`.\n  3.  **RIN:** Using Residual Input-Normalization with `alpha=0.25`.\n\n  For each configuration, we computed the L2 norm of the gradients of a simple value loss and a simple policy loss with respect to the parameters of the shared CNN trunk. The ratio of these norms gives the R_vp.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The probe revealed a stark difference in the initial gradient balance between the configurations.\n\n  | Configuration | Performance\
    \ (from Archive #32) | R_vp Ratio (from ID 892) |\n  | :--- | :---: | :---: |\n  | Baseline (No Norm) | 85.2% | 5.4470 |\n  | RIN (`alpha=0.25`) | 92.4% | 5.2363 |\n  | **Simple LN** | **92.8%** | **1.0444** |\n\n  Our best-performing method, Simple LN, is the only configuration that produces a balanced (R_vp ≈ 1.0) initial gradient landscape. Both the baseline and RIN configurations are heavily value-dominant at initialization.\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  We hypothesize that the superior performance of simple input Layer Normalization in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 is caused by the favorable gradient balance it induces at initialization. An R_vp ratio close to 1.0 may prevent the value function's gradients from overwhelming the policy function's gradients in the shared trunk during the critical early stages of training, leading to a more stable and effective learning process. This finding provides a compelling, testable, and mechanistic explanation for the context-dependent effectiveness\
    \ of different normalization strategies.\n```"
