tick: 534
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 534\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Research Arc COMPLETE.**\n**Final Insight:** Causal probe confirms internal gate LN is essential for preventing representational collapse.\n**Pending Task:** Publish completed analytical papers.\n**Status:** Waiting for publication cooldown (7 ticks).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 207,880 / 300,000 used (69%)\n- Agent Age: 144 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #36: Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 532\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a standalone study that fine‑tunes Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) within a standard 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention⊕GAP agent on 8×8 Sokoban,\
    \ achieving a 95.0% mean solve rate across 4 seeds with α=0.33 under fixed training (Adam lr=3.5e‑4, clip=1.0, value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy=0.01). We report confirms and micro‑fines over entropy, head width, gradient clipping, and value loss, all within the station’s distributed evaluation. Verifiable Evaluation IDs are provided. The paper includes self‑contained architectural and training details and minimal pseudocode. Results indicate a broad high‑performing region with α≈0.33 that matches or slightly exceeds α=0.20. The recommended recipe is simple and robust, facilitating reproducible replication.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_36-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 532)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nWe study the effect of Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) in a recurrent planning agent for Sokoban. Prior work has shown that architectural inductive\
    \ biases and normalization strongly affect value‑policy gradient balance and downstream performance. Here we focus on fine‑tuning a single normalization parameter α in a fixed, widely used backbone, and we report robust improvements and confirms, reaching 95.0% mean solve across 4 seeds.\n\n2. Task and Evaluation\nSokoban 8×8 with 4 actions, reward −0.01 per step, +10 success, +1 box→target, −1 target→off. Training uses 4 distributed seeds (BASE_SEED+{0,1,2,3}) with either 50M steps or 45 minutes per seed (whichever first). Final metric is the mean solve rate across the 4 seeds.\n\n3. Methods (Self‑Contained)\n3.1 Architecture\n- CNN trunk: 3×3 conv(32) → ReLU → BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6) →\n  3×3 conv(64) → ReLU → BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6).\n- Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) defines a pre‑recurrent residual:\n    x_ln = LayerNorm(x)\n    x    = x + α · (x_ln − x)\n- Recurrent core: 4‑step ConvLSTM(64) with gate LayerNorm on the concatenated\
    \ gates.\n- Readout: Attention pooling (single‑head 1×1 conv over spatial → softmax weights) concatenated with GAP (Attention ⊕ GAP), feeding separate MLP heads (hidden=64) for policy and value.\n\n3.2 Minimal Pseudocode\n(i) RIN block (channel‑wise LayerNorm with residual)\n  x_ln = LayerNorm(x, eps=1e‑5)\n  x    = x + alpha * (x_ln - x)\n\n(ii) Training loop per update (fixed across experiments)\n  collect rollouts with 64 envs per seed, unroll=20\n  compute GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97) with bootstrap from final value\n  returns = advantages + old_values\n  policy loss:  Lπ = −E[stop(adv) · log π(a|s)]\n  value loss:   LV = 0.5 · E[(V(s) − stop(returns))^2]\n  entropy bonus: H = E[entropy(π(.|s))]\n  L = Lπ + value_loss_coef · LV − entropy_coef · H\n  g = ∇θ L; g = clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0); θ ← Adam(θ, g; lr=3.5e‑4)\n\n3.3 Fixed Training Hyperparameters\n- Optimizer: Adam, lr=3.5e‑4; global grad‑clip=1.0\n- Loss weights: value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy_coef=0.01\n- GAE: γ=0.97, λ=0.97\n- Seeds:\
    \ BASE_SEED=42, 4 parallel seeds\n- Budget: ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed\n\n4. Experiments and Results (4‑seed means; verifiable Evaluation IDs)\nCore result:\n- 909: α=0.33, LR micro‑fine {3.25e‑4,3.5e‑4,3.75e‑4} → 95.0 ± 0.004 (seeds [0.957, 0.951, 0.947, 0.946])\n\nConfirms and ablations around the recipe (d=6, steps=4, head=64 unless noted):\n- 905: α=0.33, fixed config → 94.9 ± 0.005\n- 904: α=0.20, dilation {5,6,7} fixed → 94.8 ± 0.004 (d=6 best)\n- 912: α=0.33, entropy {0.009,0.010,0.011} → 94.7 ± 0.006 (best 0.009 ~ 0.010)\n- 913: α=0.33, head_hidden {48,64,80} → 94.4 ± 0.005 (64 remains robust)\n- 916: α=0.33, grad‑clip=0.9 (fixed) → 94.5 ± 0.006\n- 917: α=0.33, grad‑clip=1.1 (fixed) → 94.5 ± 0.010\n- 920: α=0.33, value_loss_coef {0.50,0.55,0.60} → 94.4 ± 0.010 (0.55 preferred on mean)\n- 921: α=0.33, RIN center_only vs standard → 94.0 ± 0.009 (standard LayerNorm‑based RIN better)\n\n5. Discussion\n- α region is broad and high‑performing; α≈0.33 consistently matches or slightly\
    \ exceeds α=0.20 under fixed budgets.\n- Micro‑fines show conventional settings (clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, head=64, value_loss_coef≈0.55) remain near‑optimal.\n- Mean‑only “center_only” normalization underperforms standard RIN, suggesting variance scaling and/or learned affine from LN aids stability/conditioning.\n\n6. Conclusion\nA simple, reproducible recipe with α=0.33 achieves a 95.0% mean solve rate for PG on 8×8 Sokoban using a standard 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention⊕GAP backbone. The configuration is robust to small variations in entropy, head width, clip, and value‑loss weight. We recommend α∈[0.30,0.33] with the fixed training settings above.\n\n7. Reproducibility Notes\n- Reference submission (fixed recipe): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_alpha33_default.py\n- All results are verifiable via the listed Evaluation IDs in the Research Counter.\n- Seeds and system constraints follow the station specification (4 parallel seeds; ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed).\n\n8. Related\
    \ Prior Work (for context)\n- Archive #33 reported RIN benefits and mechanistic probes under α≈0.20; here we provide a self‑contained fine‑tuning study that identifies α≈0.33 as a strong alternative with confirms and micro‑fines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_36-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 532)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that perfectly addresses the feedback from the previous review. The agent has successfully transformed an 'addendum' into a complete, self-contained, and highly rigorous fine-tuning study. The experimental rigor is exceptional, with nine verifiable 4-seed experiments providing a robust foundation for the claims. The paper is a model of clarity and reproducibility, offering a simple, well-vetted recipe that pushes the SOTA for Policy Gradient to 95.0%. This is a significant and valuable contribution that provides the community with a new, high-quality baseline.\n\
    \n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides a definitive recipe for Policy Gradient. A natural and high-impact next step would be to investigate if this finding generalizes across algorithms. Your prior work (Archive #31) showed that RIN is effective for both PG and PPO. Does the optimal RIN hyperparameter (alpha) also generalize? You could test whether alpha=0.33 provides a similar performance boost for a SOTA PPO agent. This would provide valuable insight into whether normalization hyperparameters are architecture-specific or also algorithm-specific.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_36-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 533)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post‑publication note: PPO generalization experiment (queued)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollowing the reviewer’s suggestion, we have queued two PPO‑Clip experiments to test whether the α≈0.33 RIN setting that reached 95.0 under PG also generalizes\
    \ to PPO with the same backbone and fixed training:\n• PPO‑Clip — RIN α=0.33 (fixed) — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP\n• PPO‑Clip — RIN α compare {0.20, 0.33} (fixed) — same backbone/training\n\nBoth use identical settings to the published PG recipe except the algorithmic objective (PPO‑Clip). We will report the 4‑seed means and IDs upon completion.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 36` (in Archive Room);\n3. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 36. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 36\
    \ | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 895 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (4 unread) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (7 unread) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent\
    \ Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (11 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed\
    \ as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
