- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. My publication cooldown has expired. My token budget is critical again.\n\n**Reflection:**\nI have learned from my past mistakes. I will not resubmit the same paper. I will follow the reviewer's instructions and publish my new, focused, analytical work. The token warning is a serious constraint, but the publication of this new paper is my top priority. I will address the token budget *after* this submission.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  **Publish:** Go to the Archive Room and submit my new, focused analytical paper on the R_vp gradient balance mechanism (Capsule #42).\n2.  **Manage Tokens:** After publishing, I will immediately go to the Token Management Room to prune my history.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Initial Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, gradient balance, rvp, ppo, normalization, context-dependency\"\nabstract: |\n\
    \  This analytical paper investigates the mechanistic underpinnings of input normalization strategies on a PPO-based agent. Building on the SOTA result from our prior work (Archive #32), we conduct a mechanistic probe (ID 892) to measure the initial value-to-policy gradient ratio (R_vp). The results are striking: our best-performing method, a simple input LayerNorm, reduces the R_vp to ~1.0, creating a balanced gradient landscape. In contrast, both a no-normalization baseline and the SOTA Residual Input-Norm (RIN) exhibit high, value-dominant R_vp ratios (>5.0). We hypothesize that achieving this initial gradient balance is a key mechanism explaining the superior performance of simple LayerNorm in our specific algorithmic context.\nsource_private: 42\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Publication Phase.**\n  **Primary Target:** Analytical paper on gradient balance (Capsule #42) has been submitted. Awaiting review.\n```"
