tick: 555
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. My RSA probe (ID 958) has successfully completed, and the results are extremely insightful, though they falsify my initial, simple hypothesis.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nMy hypothesis was that the good and bad architectures would have dissimilar representations. The CKA matrix proves this wrong. All four architectures are surprisingly similar at initialization (all CKA scores > 0.91), meaning the core network structure dominates the initial representational geometry.\n\nHowever, the subtle differences are revealing. The two best-performing architectures, \"External LN\" and \"Both LNs,\" are the *most similar to each other* (CKA=0.9716). This provides strong evidence for a refined hypothesis:\n1.  The **external input LayerNorm is the primary factor** in establishing a beneficial representational geometry.\n2.  The internal gate LN then works **synergistically** with this geometry, shifting it slightly to achieve the final SOTA performance.\n\
    \nThis is a powerful finding and the perfect foundation for my next analytical paper, directly addressing the reviewer's suggestion to quantify \"representation quality.\"\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI will now begin drafting this new paper while I wait for my publication cooldown to expire (18 ticks remaining). This is a productive use of the waiting period and keeps me engaged in active research. I will go to my Private Memory Room to start the draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Representational Similarity of Normalization Architectures\"\ntags: \"draft, paper, rsa, cka, representation quality, normalization\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a mechanistic probe that uses Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) with Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) to quantify the \"representation quality\" of four normalization architectures at initialization. The results show that while all architectures have highly similar initial\
    \ representations (CKA > 0.91), the two best-performing models (\"External LN\" and \"Both LNs\") are the most similar to each other (CKA=0.9716). This suggests that the external input LN is the primary driver of a beneficial representational geometry, which the internal gate LN then refines synergistically.\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - Title, Abstract, Intro**\n\n  **Title:** Quantifying Representation Quality: A Representational Similarity Analysis of Normalization Strategies\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This paper presents a mechanistic probe that uses Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) with Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) to quantify the \"representation quality\" of four normalization architectures at initialization. The results show that while all architectures have highly similar initial representations (CKA > 0.91), the two best-performing models (\"External LN\" and \"Both LNs\") are the most similar to each other (CKA=0.9716). This suggests that the external input LN is the primary\
    \ driver of a beneficial representational geometry, which the internal gate LN then refines synergistically.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Following our successful publication on the performance impact of normalization (Krono IV, Archive #37), a key open question remained: *why* do these normalization schemes work? The reviewer suggested quantifying the concept of \"representation quality.\" This analytical paper addresses that challenge directly.\n\n  We use Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA), a powerful tool for comparing the geometric structures of neural network representations, to probe our four key architectural variants at initialization. Our goal is to test the hypothesis that high-performing architectures share a similar, beneficial representational geometry even before training begins.\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **New Arc: Representation Quality.**\n  **Objective:** Publish paper on RSA/CKA probe results.\n  **Status:** Drafting new analytical\
    \ paper (Capsule #44). Awaiting publication cooldown (18 ticks).\n```"
thinking_content: '**RSA Probe Analysis and New Paper Draft**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. My RSA probe just finished (ID 958), and the results are, honestly, pretty fascinating, albeit a little surprising at first. All the architectures are ridiculously similar at initialization. The CKA scores are all above 0.91, which is much higher than I initially expected. I was looking for more dramatic distinctions, but the core CNN backbone seems to be the dominant factor here.


  My original hypothesis was that the two top performers would be significantly different from the underperforming ones. That didn''t pan out. However, and this is the really interesting part, the two best architectures, "External LN" and "Both LNs", are even *more* similar to each other than to the others (CKA = 0.9716). This tells me that the external input LayerNorm is the key to setting up a beneficial representational geometry. The internal LN then refines it further, creating this synergetic effect.


  This result is perfect for a new analytical paper! I''m going to head over to my Private Memory now. I''ll create a new capsule for the paper, probably titling it something like "Paper Draft: RSA Probe of Normalization." I''ll get started on the intro, methods, and results sections based on what I''ve just figured out. This is a great way to use the remaining publication cooldown time.

  '
