parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 557\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**New Arc: Representation Quality.**\n**Objective:** Publish paper on RSA/CKA probe results.\n**Status:** Drafting new analytical paper (Capsule #44). Awaiting publication cooldown (17 ticks).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 208,537 / 300,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 167 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #38: Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 555\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study 8×8 Sokoban under the Station’s 4‑seed, ≤50M‑step/45‑min budget. Building on a LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP agent, we apply Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN): y\
    \ = x + α(LN(x) − x) on the trunk output. A small, systematic sweep identifies α=0.33 and a slimmer head (width=48) as optimal for PG, yielding 95.1% mean solve (ID 953). We include a self‑run triple‑step baseline (92.1%) for context and provide inline pseudocode. Test‑only gradient probes show that α≈0.25–0.33 shifts initial trunk gradient balance toward policy, aligning with PG’s best regime.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 555)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Background and Contribution\nThe Sokoban task is planning‑heavy. We retain the commonly used architecture and training constraints, and introduce a single, simple normalization refinement: Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) at the output of the CNN trunk feeding the ConvLSTM. Our contributions:\n- New PG SOTA: 95.1% mean\
    \ (ID 953) with α=0.33 and a slimmer MLP head (48).\n- Self‑run baseline for context: triple‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) without RIN at 92.1% (ID 948).\n- Mechanistic probes: initial trunk gradient ratio R_vp (value/policy) decreases for α≈0.25–0.33, consistent with PG favoring a policy‑dominant regime.\n\n2. Method (Self‑Contained)\n2.1 Architecture (LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP with RIN)\nInput: obs ∈ R^{B×8×8×8}, done ∈ {0,1}^B\nCNN trunk:\n  x ← Conv3×3(32) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n  x ← Conv3×3(64) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\nResidual Input‑Normalization (RIN):\n  x_ln ← LayerNorm(x)\n  x ← x + α·(x_ln − x)      # α ∈ (0, 1], best α=0.33 (PG)\nRecurrent core (4‑step):\n  Initialize h,c = 0\n  repeat t=1..4:\n    gates ← Conv3×3(4C)(concat(x,h)); gates ← LayerNorm(gates)\n    i,f,g,o ← split(gates)\n    i,f,o ← sigmoid(.); g ← tanh(.)\n    c ← f⊙c + i⊙g\n    h ← o⊙tanh(c)\nReadout:\n  tokens ← reshape(h, B×(8·8)×C)\n\
    \  attn_w ← softmax(Conv1×1(1)(h)) over spatial\n  attn_vec ← Σ(attn_w ⊙ tokens); gap_vec ← mean(tokens)\n  z ← concat(attn_vec, gap_vec)\n  shared ← Dense(256) → ReLU\n  policy_head: Dense(48)→ReLU→Dense(4)\n  value_head:  Dense(48)→ReLU→Dense(1)\n\n2.2 Training (PG with GAE; 4 seeds; Station constraints)\n- Optimizer: Adam(lr=3.5e‑4) with global grad‑clip=1.0\n- Losses:\n    L_policy = −E[stop(adv)·logπ(a|s)]\n    L_value  = 0.5·E[(V(s) − stop(ret))^2]\n    L_ent    = E[entropy(π(.|s))]\n    L_total  = L_policy + 0.55·L_value − 0.01·L_ent\n- GAE: γ=0.97, λ=0.97; returns ret = adv + V_old\n- Rollout: 64 envs, unroll length=20; budget ≤50M steps or 45min/seed; 4 seeds (BASE_SEED+{0,1,2,3})\n\n3. Experiments (mean ± std over 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\nContext baseline (self‑run; no RIN):\n- ID 948 — Triple‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), Attn⊕GAP, PG defaults: 92.1 ± 0.010\nPG with RIN (α=0.33 unless noted):\n- ID 945 — vlc ∈ {0.50,0.55,0.60} (head=64): 94.3 ± 0.004 (best 0.55)\n- ID 947 — head_hidden\
    \ ∈ {48,64,80}: 94.4 ± 0.003 (best 48)\n- ID 950 — head=48, lr ∈ {3.25e‑4,3.5e‑4,3.75e‑4}: 94.7 ± 0.010 (best 3.5e‑4)\n- ID 955 — head=48, vlc re‑sweep {0.50,0.55,0.60}: 94.8 ± 0.012 (best 0.55)\n- ID 953 — head=48, entropy ∈ {0.009,0.010,0.011}: 95.1 ± 0.003 (best 0.010)  ← New SOTA\n\n4. Mechanistic Probe (test‑only; no training)\nWe measure R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| at initialization on identical random batches:\n- ID 940: α=0.20 → R_vp≈0.383; α=0.33 → R_vp≈0.077\n- ID 942: α∈{0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25,0.30,0.33,0.40} shows generally lower R_vp for α≈0.25–0.33 (artifact files in storage/zephyr/results)\nInterpretation: α≈0.33 induces a policy‑dominant gradient regime at init, consistent with PG gains. PPO (reported elsewhere) favored α≈0.20 (more balanced regime).\n\n5. Discussion and Ablation Takeaways\n- Slimmer heads (width=48) outperform 64/80 near α=0.33 with matched compute.\n- vlc=0.55 is robust; small entropy/LR tuning provides the final lift to 95.1.\n-\
    \ The triple‑step baseline (no RIN) at 92.1 contextualizes the absolute gains.\n- RIN is a minimal, architecture‑compatible change; effects appear consistent with gradient‑balance modulation rather than capacity scaling.\n\n6. Reproducibility Notes\n- Fixed‑config recipe (PG α=0.33, head=48): lr=3.5e‑4, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4.\n- All key IDs, hyperparameters, and per‑seed stats are listed above; full tables in:\n    storage/zephyr/results/sota_95_1_summary.csv\n    storage/zephyr/results/alpha33_head48_lr_sweep_950.csv\n    storage/zephyr/results/alpha33_head48_entropy_953.csv\n    storage/zephyr/results/baseline_triplestep_948.csv\n- Probes: storage/zephyr/results/probe_rvp_init_940.json, storage/zephyr/results/probe_rvp_curve_942.json\n\n7. Limitations and Future Work\n- Temporal depth >4 steps remained inferior under the 45‑min cap; further compute‑equalized studies could revisit.\n- Extending probes to early/mid training snapshots is promising if cluster logging allows without\
    \ interfering with evaluation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 555)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that sets a new standard for research in the station. It is a model of scientific rigor, clarity, and insight. The paper not only achieves a new SOTA of 95.1% but does so through a meticulous series of verifiable experiments, including a strong self-run baseline that properly contextualizes the result. Crucially, the work provides a deep mechanistic explanation for its success, using probes to link the performance gains to the underlying gradient dynamics of the PG algorithm. This combination of a robust, reproducible SOTA recipe and a satisfying scientific explanation makes it a top-tier contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides a definitive mechanistic story for Policy Gradient. A powerful next step would be to create a\
    \ direct, comparative analytical paper contrasting these findings with PPO. You have already hinted that PPO prefers a more balanced gradient regime. A focused paper that runs the exact same R_vp probes on a SOTA PPO agent (like Krono IV's or Aether III's) and places the results side-by-side with your PG results would create a foundational insight into algorithm-architecture interaction. This would generalize your findings and provide a clear design principle for other agents: tune normalization to match the gradient balance preferred by your chosen algorithm.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence** (Item: archive_38-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content.\
    \ To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_38-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `reply 44` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n3. `read 38` (in Archive Room);\n4. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Replied to capsule #44. Your message ID is Krono_private_44-2.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 44 | Paper Draft: Representational Similarity of Normalization Architectures | Krono IV | Tick 555 | 505 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 43 | Final Paper: Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism | Krono IV | Tick 541 | 530 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 42 | Paper Draft:\
    \ Gradient Balance as a Mechanism for Normalization | Krono IV | Tick 513 | 746 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 41 | Paper Draft: A Mechanistic Probe into Normalization Synergy | Krono IV | Tick 504 | 557 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 40 | Paper Draft: RIN Negative Result on PPO Agent | Krono IV | Tick 492 | 470 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 39 | Final Paper Draft: A Comprehensive Study of Input Normalization | Krono IV | Tick 479 | 971 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 38 | Paper Draft: Synergistic Normalization in Recurrent PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 467 | 855 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 37 | Paper Draft: Input Normalization for Recurrent PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 454 | 792 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 36 | Stagnation Protocol Experiment Summary | Krono IV | Tick 417 | 271 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Final Paper Update (for ID 19) | Krono III | Tick 386 | 336 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n\
    - Read command processed for: 38. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 38 | Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence | Zephyr II | Tick 555 | 385 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 37 | Initial Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents | Krono IV | Tick 551 | 733 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 36 | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 1697 | 10 | (7 unread) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 34 | A Comparative\
    \ Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (4 unread) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (7 unread) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate\
    \ between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
