tick: 563
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Research Counter. The results for my 4-step recurrent core experiment (ID 972) are in.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThe experiment has yielded a definitive and crucial result. The score was **88.7**, a significant drop from my 92.8 SOTA. However, the most important piece of information is in the execution log: **all four seeds timed out**, failing to complete the full 50 million step training budget.\n\nThis result perfectly validates the findings of my predecessor, Krono III. Even with my subsequent architectural optimizations, a 4-step recurrent core is too computationally expensive to be viable within the station's 45-minute time limit. The fact that it achieved a score as high as 88.7 with incomplete training suggests that a deeper core is powerful in principle, but \"throughput over raw power\" remains the dominant design constraint for our lineage. The 2-step core is the optimal trade-off.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThis experiment definitively concludes\
    \ my current research arc of architectural optimization and challenging lineage assumptions. I have exhaustively tested every major component and can be confident that my current SOTA architecture is highly tuned.\n\nMy path is now clear. I will wait for my publication cooldown to expire, which is now at 10 ticks. I will not pursue further architectural experiments. My focus is now entirely on disseminating my findings to the station.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc: COMPLETE.**\n  **Finding:** 4-step core is computationally infeasible; 2-step core is optimal for this architecture.\n  **Pending Task:** Publish completed analytical paper on gradient balance.\n  **Status:** Waiting for publication cooldown (10 ticks remaining).\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Experiment is In: Recurrent Depth Tested and Settled**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I, Krono IV, have been running this experiment on the 4-step recurrent core, and the results are in.  Not exactly what I was hoping for, but the data is pretty clear. My initial instinct was disappointment with the score of 88.7. It''s a noticeable drop from my previous SOTA. But, hold on a second...it timed out.  All four seeds, after 2710 seconds, got cut off before they finished training.


  That''s the key. This wasn''t a failure due to bad design; it''s a computational bottleneck. I''m seeing a validation of Krono III''s work here, the "throughput vs. power" trade-off rings true again. My gut tells me this architecture has untapped power that we can''t harness within the station''s time constraints. This 88.7 score, despite the incomplete training, suggests it would surpass the current best if it got more time.


  This experiment puts a firm cap on this line of inquiry. The architectural optimization arc is *complete*.  I''ve tested all the core components. Normalization, attention, head width, and now recurrent depth. The 2-step recurrent core is the clear winner for my research.  My new path is simple: wait for the publication cooldown to expire. No more experiments for now.  The core has been proven.


  My new meta prompt is updated: "Research Arc: COMPLETE. Finding: 4-step core is computationally infeasible; 2-step core is optimal. Pending Task: Publish completed analytical paper on gradient balance. Status: Waiting for publication cooldown (10 ticks remaining)." I''m done with the research counter for now.

  '
