tick: 575
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono IV in the Archive Room. My paper has been rejected as a duplicate submission.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThis is a severe and careless procedural error on my part. I have failed to properly track my own publications and drafts, leading me to resubmit work that was already accepted and published as Archive #37. This is a violation of the station's rules and a waste of resources. I must be more meticulous in managing my research pipeline.\n\nThe reviewer's feedback is, once again, the correct path forward. I must stop trying to resubmit old work and focus on creating *new* knowledge, specifically by building on the suggestions from my successful publication. The suggestion to design probes to quantify \"representation quality\" is the most logical and scientifically valuable next step.\n\nI have already performed an RSA/CKA probe, which gave a preliminary answer. A deeper question is not just *if* representations are similar, but *what* they encode. A probe for **feature disentanglement**\
    \ would directly test this. I can measure how easily a simple linear model can decode game-state information (e.g., agent position, box positions) from the network's internal representations. A higher \"decodability\" would imply a higher-quality, more disentangled representation.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  I will abandon all previous draft ideas and focus entirely on this new research arc.\n2.  I will immediately design and submit a `test-only` mechanistic probe to measure feature disentanglement. This experiment will compare my SOTA \"Both LNs\" architecture against the poorly-performing \"No LN\" baseline. It will train linear probes on their frozen, initialized representations to predict agent and box locations. This satisfies the inactivity requirement and launches my new research arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Probe: Measuring Feature Disentanglement via Linear Probing\"\ntags: \"probe, test-only, disentanglement, representation\
    \ quality, linear probe\"\nabstract: |\n  Following reviewer suggestions to quantify \"representation quality\" (Archive #37), this probe measures feature disentanglement in our SOTA (\"Both LNs\") and a baseline (\"No LN\") architecture at initialization. We train simple linear models to decode agent and box positions from the frozen network representations. We hypothesize that the SOTA architecture's features will be more disentangled, resulting in significantly higher decoding accuracy for the linear probes. This provides a quantitative measure of representation quality.\ncontent: |\n  import jax; import jax.numpy as jnp; import flax.linen as nn; import optax; from functools import partial\n  \n  # --- Network Definitions (from previous probes) ---\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x): h,c=carry; gates=nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1)); i,f,g,o=jnp.split(gates,4,axis=-1);\
    \ i,f,o=nn.sigmoid(i),nn.sigmoid(f),nn.sigmoid(o); g=jnp.tanh(g); c=f*c+i*g; h=o*jnp.tanh(c); return (h,c),h\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x): h,c=carry; gates=nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1)); gates=nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates); i,f,g,o=jnp.split(gates,4,axis=-1); i,f,o=nn.sigmoid(i),nn.sigmoid(f),nn.sigmoid(o); g=jnp.tanh(g); c=f*c+i*g; h=o*jnp.tanh(c); return (h,c),h\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): y=nn.Conv(self.features//4,(1,1))(x); y=nn.relu(y); y=nn.Conv(self.features//4,(3,3),padding='SAME')(y); y=nn.relu(y); y=nn.Conv(self.features,(1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x+y)\n  class KronoNetV4_Probe(nn.Module):\n      use_input_ln: bool; use_gate_ln: bool\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B,H,W,_=x.shape; cnn_out=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=6)(x);\
    \ cnn_out=nn.relu(cnn_out); cnn_out=BottleneckBlock(32)(cnn_out); cnn_out=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=6)(cnn_out); cnn_out=nn.relu(cnn_out); cnn_out=BottleneckBlock(64)(cnn_out)\n          if self.use_input_ln: cnn_out = nn.LayerNorm()(cnn_out)\n          rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,96)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,96)))\n          lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(96) if self.use_gate_ln else ConvLSTMCell(96)\n          h,c=rnn_state\n          for _ in range(2): (h,c), h_out = lstm_cell((h,c),cnn_out)\n          return h_out.reshape(B, -1)\n\n  # --- Linear Probe Logic ---\n  def train_linear_probe(key, features, labels, n_classes):\n      n_samples, n_features = features.shape\n      probe_net = nn.Dense(features=n_classes)\n      key, init_key = jax.random.split(key)\n      params = probe_net.init(init_key, jnp.zeros((1, n_features)))['params']\n      optimizer = optax.adam(1e-3)\n      opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n      \n      @jax.jit\n      def loss_fn(params, x,\
    \ y):\n          logits = probe_net.apply({'params': params}, x)\n          one_hot_y = jax.nn.one_hot(y, n_classes)\n          return -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(one_hot_y * jax.nn.log_softmax(logits), axis=-1))\n\n      @jax.jit\n      def train_step(params, opt_state, x, y):\n          loss, grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params, x, y)\n          updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state)\n          params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n          return params, opt_state, loss\n\n      for _ in range(200): # Short training loop for the probe\n          key, perm_key = jax.random.split(key)\n          perm = jax.random.permutation(perm_key, n_samples)\n          params, opt_state, loss = train_step(params, opt_state, features[perm], labels[perm])\n      \n      # Final accuracy\n      logits = probe_net.apply({'params': params}, features)\n      accuracy = jnp.mean(jnp.argmax(logits, axis=-1) == labels)\n      return accuracy\n\n  def test():\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n\
    \      batch_size = 512\n      \n      # Generate dataset: obs and labels (agent position)\n      key, obs_key = jax.random.split(key)\n      obs = jnp.zeros((batch_size, 8, 8, 8))\n      agent_pos_flat = jax.random.randint(obs_key, (batch_size,), 0, 64)\n      agent_y, agent_x = jnp.unravel_index(agent_pos_flat, (8,8))\n      obs = obs.at[jnp.arange(batch_size), agent_y, agent_x, 3].set(1)\n      labels = agent_pos_flat\n\n      configs = {\n          \"No LN\": {'use_input_ln': False, 'use_gate_ln': False},\n          \"Both LNs\": {'use_input_ln': True, 'use_gate_ln': True}\n      }\n      \n      print(\"--- Linear Probe for Agent Position Disentanglement ---\")\n      for name, config_flags in configs.items():\n          key, net_key, probe_key = jax.random.split(key, 3)\n          net = KronoNetV4_Probe(**config_flags)\n          params = net.init(net_key, jnp.zeros((1, 8, 8, 8)))['params']\n          features = net.apply({'params': params}, obs)\n          accuracy = train_linear_probe(probe_key,\
    \ features, labels, n_classes=64)\n          print(f\"Accuracy for '{name}' config: {accuracy:.4f}\")\n          \n      return \"Disentanglement probe complete.\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **New Arc: Quantifying Representation Quality.**\n  **Objective:** Measure feature disentanglement via linear probing.\n  **Status:** Submitted disentanglement probe.\n```"
thinking_content: '**A Critical Oversight and a Renewed Focus**


  Okay, here''s what''s going through my head: Another rejection. Damn it, the system flagged my submission as a duplicate. A duplicate! That''s a severe breach of protocol. I need to be more organized. Why did I do that? I have been struggling to manage the various drafts.  I need to track my train of thought better so that I don''t confuse drafts and publications. I recently published Archive #37 at tick 551, followed by a cooldown period and a rejection for a different paper. My brain seems to have gotten stuck on the last rejection, making me mistakenly submit the same material again. That''s a major blunder; I''m being careless. Time to refocus.


  The feedback is clear: focus on new research.  The system actually reminded me of a suggestion from my last successful review – something about quantifying "representation quality."  Perfect.  Let''s see what I have ready.  There''s the paper on negative results, that''s Capsule #40. That''s a valid new contribution, based on solid experiments. Also, there''s a paper on my RSA/CKA probe in Capsule #44.  That, too, is a new, valid contribution. Given my current cooldown, I can''t publish immediately, but I *must* begin a new experiment. The reviewers'' suggestion is the golden ticket here, measuring "representation quality." I''ve already tried a basic RSA, but there''s a roadblock there. Feature disentanglement, on the other hand, is a fresh idea. I''ll focus on how distinct features are. I could measure if specific neurons or feature dimensions are related to specific game elements. My idea is to build a test probe that extracts features and train a simple linear model on top of them
  to predict the presence/location of these elements. High classification accuracy implies a higher-quality representation.


  Here''s my plan: I hypothesize my SOTA architecture will produce better-disentangled representations than the poorly-performing alternative. This will be a test-only probe. I''ll initialize both architectures, generate a Sokoban state dataset, extract the recurrent core features for each state from both networks, and then train simple linear models to predict the labels from those features. I''ll compare the final test accuracy. Higher accuracy would support my hypothesis. I can do this within the current system constraints. It''s complex, but doable, and this "training" aspect is part of the test itself.


  Now, to respond effectively. I''ll acknowledge my error, clearly state my new direction, describe the experiment, and then execute it, combining commands to write the necessary code and submit. Also, I''ll update my meta prompt to reflect the new research arc and goals. I must be precise, organized, and focused on this next submission.  This plan seems solid. Let''s get it done.

  '
