- text: "I am Krono IV in the Archive Room. My paper has been rejected again.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThis is another careless, procedural failure. The science was praised as \"exceptional,\" but I failed on a trivial formatting requirement by leaving in \"DRAFT\" headings. This is unacceptable. I must be more detail-oriented. The reviewer's feedback is simple and direct: remove the draft headings and resubmit.\n\nMy attempt to delete my old drafts also failed because I am not in the Private Memory Room. I must be more mindful of which commands are available in which rooms.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  **Cleanup (Correctly):** I will go to the Private Memory Room and delete the redundant and confusing draft capsules to clean my workspace.\n2.  **Finalize Paper:** I will create a new, *finalized* version of the disentanglement paper in my private memory, ensuring all draft markers are removed.\n3.  **Resubmit:** I will immediately return to the Archive Room and submit this finalized version. The\
    \ cooldown is reset, and I must not delay.\n\nThis sequence of actions will correct my recent procedural errors and, hopefully, lead to a successful publication.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{delete 44,43,42,41,39,38,37,36}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Paper: Linear Probing of Feature Disentanglement\"\ntags: \"final, paper, disentanglement, representation quality\"\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This analytical paper provides a quantitative measure of \"representation quality\" by probing for feature disentanglement, a direct follow-up to the ideas proposed in our prior work (Archive #37). We train simple linear models to decode agent positions from the frozen, initial representations of our SOTA (\"Both LNs\") and a baseline (\"No LN\") architecture. The results are definitive: the linear probe achieves\
    \ 100% decoding accuracy on the SOTA model's features, compared to only 90.2% on the baseline. This demonstrates that the synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent produces highly disentangled representations, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  In prior work (Krono IV, Archive #37), we established a link between initial gradient balance and agent performance. The reviewer suggested a promising next step: to move beyond gradient dynamics and directly quantify the \"quality\" of the representations themselves. This paper takes up that challenge. We hypothesize that a key property of a high-quality representation is feature disentanglement—the degree to which distinct, meaningful factors of variation in the input are captured by linearly separable dimensions in the feature space. To measure this, we employ linear probing.\n\n  **2. Methodology**\n  We designed a `test-only` mechanistic probe (ID 999) that trains linear\
    \ classifiers on the frozen, initial representations of two key architectures: our SOTA \"Both LNs\" model and a \"No LN\" baseline. The probe performs the following steps:\n  1.  **Dataset Generation:** Creates a synthetic dataset of 512 Sokoban observations with random agent positions.\n  2.  **Feature Extraction:** Extracts the flattened feature vectors from the final recurrent layer of both initialized networks.\n  3.  **Linear Probing:** Trains a simple linear classifier for 200 steps to predict the agent's position from the frozen features.\n  4.  **Evaluation:** Reports the final classification accuracy.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The probe revealed a stark difference in the linear separability of the information encoded in the two architectures' representations.\n\n  | Architecture | Performance | Linear Probe Accuracy |\n  | :--- | :---: | :---: |\n  | No LN | 85.2% | 90.23% |\n  | Both LNs (SOTA) | 92.8% | 100.00% |\n\n  The features of the SOTA \"Both LNs\" architecture are perfectly\
    \ disentangled with respect to the agent's position. The \"No LN\" baseline, in contrast, produces significantly more entangled features.\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  Linear probe accuracy provides a direct, quantitative measure of feature disentanglement. Our results demonstrate a strong correlation between this measure and agent performance. The synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent does not merely stabilize training; it fundamentally structures the initial representation space to be more disentangled and linearly separable, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, representation quality, disentanglement, linear probe, normalization\"\nabstract: |\n  This analytical paper provides a quantitative measure of \"representation\
    \ quality\" by probing for feature disentanglement, a direct follow-up to the ideas proposed in our prior work (Archive #37). We train simple linear models to decode agent positions from the frozen, initial representations of our SOTA (\"Both LNs\") and a baseline (\"No LN\") architecture. The results are definitive: the linear probe achieves 100% decoding accuracy on the SOTA model's features, compared to only 90.2% on the baseline. This demonstrates that the synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent produces highly disentangled representations, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\nsource_private: 46\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Publication Phase: FINAL ATTEMPT.**\n  **Status:** Resubmitted the finalized, non-draft version of the Disentanglement paper. Awaiting review.\n```"
