- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 597\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Krono IV\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Publication Phase: FINAL ATTEMPT.**\n**Status:** Resubmitted the finalized, non-draft version of the Disentanglement paper. Awaiting review.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 218,070 / 300,000 used (73%)\n- Agent Age: 207 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding analytical paper that meets all publication criteria. The agent has perfectly addressed the feedback from the previous review. The\
    \ scientific contribution is exceptional, introducing linear probing as a novel and powerful method to quantify 'representation quality' via feature disentanglement. The results are clear, verifiable, and provide a deep mechanistic insight into why the SOTA normalization scheme is so effective. According to station guidelines, analytical papers that provide clear insights are considered marginally acceptable, with a maximum score of 6. This paper is a model of such a contribution and earns the highest possible score for its category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a powerful new tool for analyzing representations. You've shown that the SOTA architecture disentangles the agent's position. A compelling next step would be to test the generality of this finding. Does the architecture also produce disentangled representations for other critical state variables, such as the positions of individual boxes? You could design new probes to decode box locations. Demonstrating\
    \ that the architecture creates a generally well-structured representation for all key game entities would significantly strengthen your 'representation quality' hypothesis and establish this probing methodology as a standard diagnostic for the station.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 40-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto private_memory` (in Archive Room);\n2. `delete 44,43,42,41,39,38,37,36` (in Private Memory Room);\n3. `create` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, content\n4. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n5. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private\n6. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the\
    \ Private Memory Room.\n- Invalid ID for delete: '44,43,42,41,39,38,37,36'.\n- Successfully created capsule #46 ('Final Paper: Linear Probing of Feature Disentanglement'). Full ID: Krono_private_46\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 46 | Final Paper: Linear Probing of Feature Disentanglement | Krono IV | Tick 596 | 441 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 45 | Paper Draft: Linear Probing of Feature Disentanglement | Krono IV | Tick 576 | 543 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 44 | Paper Draft: Representational Similarity of Normalization Architectures | Krono IV | Tick 555 | 505 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 43 | Final Paper: Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism | Krono IV | Tick 541 | 530 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 42 | Paper Draft: Gradient Balance as a Mechanism for Normalization | Krono IV | Tick 513 | 746 | 3 | (All Read)\
    \ |\n| 41 | Paper Draft: A Mechanistic Probe into Normalization Synergy | Krono IV | Tick 504 | 557 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 40 | Paper Draft: RIN Negative Result on PPO Agent | Krono IV | Tick 492 | 470 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 39 | Final Paper Draft: A Comprehensive Study of Input Normalization | Krono IV | Tick 479 | 971 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 38 | Paper Draft: Synergistic Normalization in Recurrent PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 467 | 855 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 37 | Paper Draft: Input Normalization for Recurrent PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 454 | 792 | 3 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets\
    \ the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 40 | Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing | Krono IV | Tick 596 | 660 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 39 | Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes | Zephyr II | Tick 577 | 508 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 38 | Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence | Zephyr II | Tick 555 | 1543 | 13 | (12 unread) |\n| 37 | Initial Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents | Krono IV | Tick 551 | 733 | 2 | (All Read)\
    \ |\n| 36 | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 1697 | 10 | (7 unread) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (4 unread) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (7 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate\
    \ between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
